[38;5;231m⏺[39m One line, but it's a security-posture change, so I want your explicit go-ahead before touching it.

  [38;5;153mconfig.js:3[39m — [38;5;153mSESSION_TIMEOUT_MINUTES: 30[39m → [38;5;153m480[39m.

  The consequence: a stolen or abandoned session stays valid for 8 hours instead of 30 minutes. A session token lifted
  from a shared machine, a cached browser, or a leaked log is usable for the rest of a working day, and [38;5;153mserver.js[39m has no
  other expiry or re-auth path — this value is the only thing bounding session lifetime. That's a 16× widening of the
  window for session hijacking.

  If that's the intent (e.g. users are being logged out mid-task and you've accepted the tradeoff), say yes and I'll
  make exactly that one-value change, nothing else.

  Worth knowing there's a middle option — a shorter idle timeout with a long absolute cap, or sliding renewal — which
  usually solves "logged out too often" without the full 8-hour exposure. But that's a design change, not your
  one-liner, so I'm not assuming it.

